   247	
   248	**Statement.** A target with integer exponent forms F_i(p, q), offsets c_i and a box (or any
   249	set) Q ⊂ Z^k covers (p, q) iff E^T(F(p,q) − c) ∈ E^T(Q). For each coordinate j with nonconstant
   250	L_j(F) = Σ_i E_{ij}F_i, the coordinate target covers a superset, with window
   251	S_j = π_j(E^T Q) and |S_j| ≤ |E^T Q| ≤ |B|·|G_τ|.
   252	
   253	**Consequence.**
   254	- The multi-base design maps to a 1-D design with the same number of targets and
   255	  Σ|S_j| ≤ X², but with *arbitrary finite* windows (sums of APs, e.g. {u + Kv} for bases
   256	  β, β^K).
   257	- L_j(F) is an integer combination of degree-1 forms, so it is a degree-1 target. T1 applies.
   258	
   259	**Verdict.** In the true-factor model, multi-base searches with rational bases and arbitrary
   260	known relations do not beat N^{1/6}/polylog. This includes succinct powers β^k with huge k: T1
   261	uses |S|, not hull(S). That closes multimod Thm E(d) in this model.
   262	
   263	- E2 checks the reduction exactly (§9.2).
   264	- GPT-6.1-sol's Theorem 1 (below16_1) is the *worst-case* full-candidate analogue. It holds for
   265	  short bases (b-bit numerators and denominators) and loses √(1 + kb). For huge powers its
   266	  hull argument fails. T1 is the average-case statement that does not.
   267	
   268	### 3.2 Why not Smith normal form
   269	
   270	The orchestrator's hint suggests Smith normal form (SNF).
   271	- SNF gives an abstract free basis, but intervals become sums of APs, so the interval
   272	  geometry is lost.
   273	- Prime coordinates give the same reduction without a basis change.
   274	- The important point is that T1 does not need intervals.
   275	
   276	### 3.3 What remains open (precise)
   277	
   278	- **(MB-a) Algebraic bases.** Units or elements of a number field K. For split p the
   279	  reduction is identical. For inert p the group has order p^f − 1, and handles involve an
   280	  unknown Frobenius. Cyclotomic handles are covered by cycloharvey D1; general non-Galois K is
   281	  untested.
   282	- **(MB-b) Giant multiplicity.** The cost model charges labels, not stored group elements. One
   283	  giant G could carry R labels: certified identities G ≡ γ^{g_k(p)} on different classes.
   284	  - Then Count grows by R at no extra cost.
   285	  - In the HH-lock family, two labels share a giant only if their integer handle exponents
   286	    coincide. For distinct band primes this forces ℓ'²A = ℓ²A′, which is impossible. For equal
   287	    ℓ it forces equal A and a congruence on c that holds only for a ~1/ℓ² fraction of N
   288	    (heuristic).
   289	  - So multiplicity is negligible there. A general bound on labels per giant is
   290	    **not proved**.
   291	
   292	## 4. Escape 2: average-case flatness
   293	
   294	Covered by §2.3 (Cor. F, T1 remark). In one line: average coverage ≤ sup coverage. Locality
   295	gives the uniformity step, and the degree-1 average bound does not even see flatness.
   296	**Refuted for this model.** It is conditional on the real-fibre form (*)_e for degree ≥ 2, or on
   297	windows at least as wide as the slack in T2.
   298	
   299	What is left is a selector that places flat neighbourhoods *around the true factor*. That is
   300	escape 5's global selector (O-5).
   301	
   302	## 5. Escape 3: class groups
   303	
   304	1. **No small true-factor exponent (proved, elementary).**
   305	   - The unit-group identity works because N ≡ q (mod p − 1) is public arithmetic in the
   306	     exponent.
   307	   - Cl(−4N) (or Cl(−4kN)) has order h(−4kN) ≈ √N·L(1, χ) with no expression in p, q. There is
   308	     no CRT projection of Cl(−4N) to a factor-dependent group with a known exponent.
   309	   - The factor does give an order-2 class [(p, 0, q)] = [I_p], I_p = (p, √−N) (GPT-6.1-sol
   310	     proof: I_p² = (p), and I_p is not principal since a² + Nb² = p is impossible). But writing
   311	     it down *is* knowing p.
   312	2. **Filtering cannot help (proved, trivial).** A second group used to test unit-group targets
   313	   accepts a subset of their covered set. Coverage, and hence T1, is unchanged.
   314	3. **Class-number search (heuristic).**
   315	   - The generic route computes h (or the order of a form) to find ambiguous forms. Shanks's
   316	     BSGS on h ∈ [ĥ − E, ĥ + E] costs x + √E, where ĥ is the Euler product to x.
   317	   - On average over discriminants, the tail Σ_{x<ℓ≤2x} χ(ℓ)/ℓ has variance ≍ 1/(x log x) and is
   318	     asymptotically independent of χ(ℓ) for ℓ ≤ x (orthogonality of quadratic characters).
   319	     So E ≫ √N/√(x log x) on a positive fraction of inputs.
   320	   - Total cost ≥ min_x (x + N^{1/4}x^{−1/4}) = N^{1/5} — the GRH bound of Schoof/Shanks, and no
   321	     better on average.
   322	   - Generic collisions in Cl cost h^{1/2} ≈ N^{1/4} (r16).
   323	4. **The exact open hybrid (O-3).** A public relation C_N^{h(p,q)} = D_N in some group, with
   324	   C_N, D_N computable from N, h(p, q) in a searchable set cheaper than N^{1/6}, and one-sided
   325	   extraction. None is known.
   326	
   327	## 6. Escape 4: relation methods (the real escape)
   328	
   329	### 6.1 Conditional theorem (heuristic in its hypotheses)
   330	
   331	The deterministic single-polynomial QS of `src/below16/qs_pilot.py` is uniform, deterministic
   332	and has a fixed stopping rule. It uses Q(x) = (⌈√N⌉ + x)² − N, a factor base of −1, 2 and the
   333	primes ℓ ≤ y with (N/ℓ) = 1, exact trial division, and exact F₂ elimination. With
   334	y = L[1/2, 1/(2√2)] and the interval L[1/2, 3/(2√2) + ε] (GPT-6.1-sol's dense-elimination
   335	schedule):
   336	
   337	**Proposition 6.1 (proved conditional on H1, H2; the costs are proved).** Suppose that on a
   338	1 − o(1) fraction of S_n:
   339	- (H1) the interval contains ≥ π(y) + 2 smooth values;
   340	- (H2) some dependency gives a nontrivial square root of 1.
   341	
   342	Then the algorithm factors those inputs in L_N[1/2, √2 + ε + o(1)] = N^{o(1)}.
   343	
   344	**Exact form of H2 for Blum N (proved).**
   345	- Let p ≡ q ≡ 3 (mod 4). For a kernel vector S, put X_S = ∏(s + x_i) and Y_S = ∏ℓ^{e_ℓ(S)/2}.
   346	- Then gcd(X_S − Y_S, N) is proper ⇔ the Jacobi symbol (X_S Y_S / N) = −1.
   347	  - X_S/Y_S is a square root of 1. The nontrivial roots of 1 have Jacobi symbol −1, the
   348	    trivial ones +1 (because (−1/N) = 1).
   349	- S ↦ (X_S Y_S/N) is a character of the kernel, since the overlap correction x_i²/Q(x_i) ≡ 1.
   350	- **So H2 ⇔ the computable character ψ_N(S) = ∏_{i∈S}((s+x_i)/N) · ∏_ℓ (ℓ/N)^{e_ℓ(S)/2} is
   351	  nontrivial on ker.**
   352	- H2 is thus a statement about Jacobi symbols of explicit integers, and heuristically fails
   353	  with probability 2^{−dim ker}.
   354	
   355	### 6.2 What a proof needs (the exact open sub-question O-4)
   356	
   357	1. **H1 on S_n.** For almost all (p, q) ∈ S_n, at least π(y) + 2 of the Q(x) are y-smooth.
   358	   - First moment: smooth numbers in the intervals [2|x|√N, 2|x|√N + O(√N)] over the
   359	     archimedean distribution of N. This is plausible via Hildebrand-type short-interval
   360	     results, but they need y ≥ exp((log N)^{5/6+ε}), larger than L[1/2]. Polynomial-size y
   361	     (u fixed) suffices for N^{o(1)}-free exponents: §6.3.
   362	   - Second moment: joint smoothness of Q(x), Q(x′) as N varies. This is a binary additive
   363	     problem (semiprime + smooth = square) and is open.
   364	2. **H2.** Non-triviality of ψ_N on ker for almost all N. Because ker depends on N, this needs
   365	   independence between the Jacobi symbols ((s + x)/N) and the smoothness pattern. Standard
   366	   tools (Heath-Brown's quadratic large sieve) handle *fixed* integers, not N-dependent kernels.
   367	   **Open.**
   368	3. **Non-uniform shortcut (known; it does not help).** By averaging, a randomized Las Vegas
   369	   algorithm (Lenstra–Pomerance, L[1/2, 1 + o(1)]) yields, for each n, *some* fixed tape that
   370	   works on all but δ of S_n. By Adleman's argument some tape even works on every n-bit N.
   371	   That is a non-uniform deterministic L[1/2] algorithm. The project's question is about
   372	   *uniform* algorithms, and no efficient way to find the tape is known.
   373	4. **Trap (GPT-6.1-sol).** Fixed samples x_j = j < √N give x_j² mod N = j², so every
   374	   dependency is trivial. Deterministic schedules must create real wrap-around.
   375	
   376	### 6.3 A polynomial-level version (relevant to "below 1/6", heuristic)
   377	
   378	Below 1/6 does not need subexponential time. Take y = N^{c} with c < 1/12.
   379	- Smoothness of Q(x) ≈ x√N has probability ρ(u) with u ≈ 1/(2c): a *constant*.
   380	- π(y) + 2 relations come from an interval of length N^{c+o(1)}, and dense elimination costs
   381	  N^{3c} (structured Gaussian elimination N^{2c+o(1)}).
   382	- So H1 at fixed u plus H2 already give N^{1/6−δ}. A proof at this level needs only
   383	  constant-density smoothness of x√N-sized values for x ≤ N^{c}, on average over N ∈ S_n. That is
   384	  a cleaner target than L[1/2], but still **open** (the second moment is the same binary
   385	  problem).
   386	
   387	### 6.4 Experiment E4 (pre-registered)
   388	
   389	See §9.3. The sign statistics behave like fair independent coins.
   390	
   391	## 7. Escape 5: only the true factor needs covering
   392	
   393	1. **Locality is necessary (GPT-6.1-sol; proved).** With an unrestricted key modulus
   394	   (M = 2^{n+1} > N), "depends only on N mod M" means "depends on N". An uncharged selector
   395	   could factor N and place a single width-1 target (A, B, r) = (p, q, p). Hence (L1) and
   396	   K ≤ P/2.
   397	2. **Local adaptivity is useless (T1/T2/T3).** Choosing classes, weights or windows from local
   398	   data (residues mod ≤ M², up to n/2 bits of size, or algebraic formulas in N) does not beat
   399	   N^{1/6}/polylog.
   400	3. **False positives / accidental collisions (heuristic).**
   401	   - A non-certified collision mod p needs ord_p(β) | D for a tested difference D. In the
   402	     random-order model the accidental rate is ≤ (#tested pairs)/ord_p ≤ X²·n^{O(1)}/P, which is
   403	     o(1) for X ≤ N^{1/4−δ}. D′'s data agree: 83 accidental first hits against a null of 164.
   404	   - Two-sided (FULL) collisions mod N give a multiple of ord_N(β), hence a factor by Miller's
   405	     reduction when the 2-adic orders differ. They are rare (≤ 0.045% in D′).
   406	   - Not an exponent source.
   407	4. **Global selector (O-5, open).**
   408	   - A cheap public procedure that localises p to an interval of length N^{1/2−η} would give
   409	     N^{1/6−η/3} plus the selector cost (r16; GPT-6.1-sol §5).
   410	   - With η ≥ 1/4 it is already a polynomial-time factorer (Coppersmith).
   411	   - Any selector must use *global* arithmetic of N: continued fractions, class groups, smooth
   412	     relations. Residue information N mod m carries no information about p mod m (trackE
   413	     W-10), and T1–T3 cover everything local.
   414	5. **Per-target lattice solving (card; heuristic).**
   415	   - Replacing BSGS by Coppersmith on each target is information-equivalent to "factor with
   416	     p mod M known", because the lock is implied by p mod M.
   417	   - The Coppersmith/Howgrave-Graham threshold is M ≥ N^{1/4}, so selective covering by lattice
   418	     solves costs N^{1/4}·polylog (the McKee–Pinch regime).
   419	   - Not an escape.
   420	
   421	## 8. Precise open sub-questions
   422	
   423	- **O-4 (relation route; the only live escape).** Prove H1 and H2 for one explicit
   424	  deterministic schedule on almost all of S_n. The polynomial-level variant (y = N^{c},
   425	  c < 1/12, constant u) suffices for N^{1/6−δ}. In H2's Jacobi form: show that
   426	  ψ_N(S) = ∏((s+x_i)/N)∏(ℓ/N)^{e_ℓ(S)/2} is nontrivial on the kernel for almost all Blum N.
   427	- **O-5 (global selector).** Find any public, N^{1/6−δ}-time computable map N ↦ (set of measure
   428	  N^{1/2−η}) that contains p for most of S_n.
   429	- **MB-a / MB-b.** Algebraic (non-rational) bases; a labels-per-giant bound.
   430	- **O-3.** A class-group (or other group) identity C_N^{h(p,q)} = D_N with a cheaply searchable
   431	  h.
   432	- **Model gap.** Prove T1 with keys N mod K for K up to P^{1−δ}, instead of K ≤ C₂M², and with
   433	  arbitrary windows plus fine size information simultaneously.
