   236	## 3. Escape 1: several bases with known relations
   237	
   238	### 3.1 Reduction (Prop. MB; proved)
   239	
   240	**Setup.**
   241	- Let the bases α_1..α_k be positive rationals (any number, any known relations).
   242	- The relations provable for every p are exactly the relations in Q^*, since a relation false
   243	  in Q^* fails for all large p.
   244	- In prime-valuation coordinates (one per prime dividing some α_i), an identity-provable
   245	  collision of ∏α_i^{u_i} with the baby/giant product means E^T u = 0, where E is the k × r
   246	  valuation matrix.
   247	
   248	**Statement.** A target with integer exponent forms F_i(p, q), offsets c_i and a box (or any
   249	set) Q ⊂ Z^k covers (p, q) iff E^T(F(p,q) − c) ∈ E^T(Q). For each coordinate j with nonconstant
   250	L_j(F) = Σ_i E_{ij}F_i, the coordinate target covers a superset, with window
   251	S_j = π_j(E^T Q) and |S_j| ≤ |E^T Q| ≤ |B|·|G_τ|.
   252	
   253	**Consequence.**
   254	- The multi-base design maps to a 1-D design with the same number of targets and
   255	  Σ|S_j| ≤ X², but with *arbitrary finite* windows (sums of APs, e.g. {u + Kv} for bases
   256	  β, β^K).
   257	- L_j(F) is an integer combination of degree-1 forms, so it is a degree-1 target. T1 applies.
   258	
   259	**Verdict.** In the true-factor model, multi-base searches with rational bases and arbitrary
   260	known relations do not beat N^{1/6}/polylog. This includes succinct powers β^k with huge k: T1
   261	uses |S|, not hull(S). That closes multimod Thm E(d) in this model.
   262	
   263	- E2 checks the reduction exactly (§9.2).
   264	- GPT-6.1-sol's Theorem 1 (below16_1) is the *worst-case* full-candidate analogue. It holds for
   265	  short bases (b-bit numerators and denominators) and loses √(1 + kb). For huge powers its
   266	  hull argument fails. T1 is the average-case statement that does not.
   267	
   268	### 3.2 Why not Smith normal form
   269	
   270	The orchestrator's hint suggests Smith normal form (SNF).
   271	- SNF gives an abstract free basis, but intervals become sums of APs, so the interval
   272	  geometry is lost.
   273	- Prime coordinates give the same reduction without a basis change.
   274	- The important point is that T1 does not need intervals.
   275	
   276	### 3.3 What remains open (precise)
   277	
   278	- **(MB-a) Algebraic bases.** Units or elements of a number field K. For split p the
   279	  reduction is identical. For inert p the group has order p^f − 1, and handles involve an
   280	  unknown Frobenius. Cyclotomic handles are covered by cycloharvey D1; general non-Galois K is
   281	  untested.
   282	- **(MB-b) Giant multiplicity.** The cost model charges labels, not stored group elements. One
   283	  giant G could carry R labels: certified identities G ≡ γ^{g_k(p)} on different classes.
   284	  - Then Count grows by R at no extra cost.
   285	  - In the HH-lock family, two labels share a giant only if their integer handle exponents
   286	    coincide. For distinct band primes this forces ℓ'²A = ℓ²A′, which is impossible. For equal
   287	    ℓ it forces equal A and a congruence on c that holds only for a ~1/ℓ² fraction of N
   288	    (heuristic).
   289	  - So multiplicity is negligible there. A general bound on labels per giant is
   290	    **not proved**.
