    66	## 1. Models
    67	
    68	- **𝔐₁ (cycloharvey §§2, 3.4).**
    69	  - Targets are Laurent polynomials g ∈ Λ_I with I ⊆ [−d,d]∖{0}, or g ∈ L_{r,M} for
    70	    classes modulo **one** modulus M.
    71	  - Windows are integer intervals.
    72	  - Coverage must be proved by the identity for every integer candidate p in
    73	    [√(N/c), √N]: if p were a divisor, g(p) would be an integer in the window.
    74	  - Cost is X ≥ #targets with Σ W_τ ≤ X² (shared or per-run baby lists).
    75	- **𝔐⁺ (enlarged).** The same, except every target τ carries its own modulus M_τ ≥ 1,
    76	  with gcd(M_τ, N) = 1 (Lemma K′ needs it), and its own class r_τ. Both are chosen freely:
    77	  nested, coprime, any number. The cost hypotheses are unchanged:
    78	  #targets ≤ X and Σ W_τ ≤ X².
    79	  - These hold for per-modulus baby lists, a shared list, or any mix, because
    80	    Σ W_τ ≤ Σ m_{j(τ)}⌈W_τ/m_{j(τ)}⌉ ≤ X·max m ≤ X².
    81	  - The model is therefore *generous*: it does not charge the distinct-base problem that
    82	    codex r10 warns about. DCL solves that problem anyway (§2.3).
    83	- **𝔐^k (multi-base).** There are k bases α_i; a target is a handle ∏α_i^{F_i(p,q)} with a box
    84	  window ∏[U_i, U_i + W_i). Baby set B ⊂ Z^k, giant sets G_τ, and coverage needs F(p) ∈
    85	  B + G_τ, i.e. B + G_τ ⊇ box_τ, the whole box and not only the curve {F(p)}.
    86	  Cost ≥ |B| + Σ|G_τ|.
    87	
    88	## 2. The scheme: divisor-cover Lehman (DCL)
    89	
    90	### 2.1 Class lock (proved; HH Prop. 3.3)
    91	
    92	Let M be a prime with M ∤ 2ABN, and let r be a root of Br² ≡ AN (mod M). Write s = N·r^{−1}
    93	(mod M²; the inverse is taken mod M², as codex points out). Then for every integer
    94	p ≡ r (mod M) with q = N/p ∈ Z:
    95	
    96	  Bp + Aq ≡ c := Br + As (mod M²).
    97	
    98	*Proof.* Write p = r + Mx. Then q ≡ s − sMx·r^{−1} (mod M²), so
    99	Bp + Aq ≡ Br + As + Mx(B − AN r^{−2}) = Br + As + Mx·(Br² − AN)/r² ≡ Br + As (mod M²). ∎
   100	
   101	So g = (Bp + Aq − c)/M² ∈ Z. Over the balanced range, F = Bp + AN/p lies in [F_lo, F_hi]:
   102	F is convex, with its minimum 2√(ABN) when A/B ∈ [1/2, 1] and an endpoint value otherwise.
   103	The whole-range window is therefore g ∈ [⌈(F_lo−c)/M²⌉, ⌊(F_hi−c)/M²⌋]. Its width is
   104	W ≤ (F_hi − F_lo)/M² + 1 = O(H√N/M² + 1), with H = max(A,B).
   105	
   106	The exact check is `dcl.verify_windows`: rational arithmetic, convexity, both class
   107	endpoints and the class members around the stationary point. It is run on every
   108	experimental design and records 0 violations.
   109	
   110	### 2.2 Coverage is a sieve
   111	
   112	With whole-range windows, target (v, M, r) covers exactly the integers p ≡ r (mod M) in the
   113	range. Since Bp² − AN = p(Bp − Aq), the condition p ≡ r for some root r is the same as
   114	M | Bp² − AN, which for an actual divisor is M | Bp − Aq. So:
   115	
   116	  **p is covered ⇔ some chosen (v, M) has M | Bp² − AN** (equivalently M | Bp − Aq).
   117	
   118	The algorithm never guesses. Its target set is determined by N, and failure happens
   119	only when no chosen (v, M) divides.
   120	
   121	### 2.3 One baby list for all moduli (proved)
   122	
   123	Let L = ∏_{M used} M² and γ = β^L. Since p ≡ 1 and q ≡ N (mod p − 1),
   124	
   125	  (β^{L/M²})^{B + AN − c} ≡ β^{(L/M²)(Bp + Aq − c)} = β^{L g} = γ^{g} (mod p).
   126	
   127	Codex verified the identity B + AN − (Bp + Aq) = (p − 1)(Aq − B).
   128	
   129	- **Shared list.** Every target is a giant step (β^{L/M²})^{B+AN−c}·γ^{−g_lo−jm} against
   130	  the single baby list γ^i.
   131	- **Answer to the r10 warning.** The baby lists of different moduli coincide as group
   132	  elements because the handle, not the base, is raised to L/M².
   133	- **Cost of the powers.** All β^{L/M²} come from a product tree in the exponent
   134	  (`operational.all_but_one_powers`) in O(log L·log #moduli) = Õ(Y) multiplications.
   135	  Every handle then costs O(log N).
   136	
   137	### 2.4 Cost and design
   138	
   139	- **Cost.** m + Σ_τ ⌈W_τ/m⌉, cycloharvey's counting.
   140	- **λ.** Each (v, M) has ρ_v(M) ∈ {0, 2} targets, and covers a fraction ρ/M of the
   141	  candidates. Write λ = Σ ρ_v(M)/M for the expected cover multiplicity.
   142	- **Efficiency.** With m given, a pair (v, M) with M ≳ (H√N/m)^{1/2} needs one giant step
   143	  per target, and its efficiency ρ/M decreases in M.
   144	- **The greedy design** (`dcl.design`) takes (v, M) pairs in decreasing order of
   145	  ρ/(M⌈W/m⌉) until λ reaches its target. `best_design` then optimises m.
   146	- **Heuristic size.** With M ≈ Y, cost ≈ m + λY·(1 + o(1)) and m ≈ √N/Y², so
   147	
   148	  cost ≈ 3·(λ/2)^{2/3}·N^{1/6}·(polylog from weight heights).
   149	
   150	### 2.5 Heuristic coverage (Poisson; conjectured)
   151	
   152	Treat the events "M | Bp − Aq" as independent across (v, M), with probabilities
   153	ρ_v(M)/M. Then a fraction ≈ e^{−λ} of the candidates is uncovered.
   154	- **R1.** λ = 3, success ≈ 95%.
   155	- **Full coverage.** Needs e^{−λ}·#candidates < 1, so λ ≈ ln √N.
   156	
   157	The exhaustive check (§6, E-C) finds coverage somewhat *better* than Poisson.
   158	- R1 leaves 1.3–4.3% uncovered, against Poisson's ≈5%.
   159	- The smallest λ giving full coverage, λ*, grows like ln √N, as the Poisson tail predicts.
   160	  It is 5.5–8 at n = 32–36 and 11–15.5 at n = 44–48, against ln √N + 2 = 13.1–18.6.
   161	
   162	## 3. Theory
   163	
   164	Notation: P = √N, and t = p/P ∈ J = [c^{−1/2}, 1]. #candidates ≍ P.
   165	
   166	### 3.1 Per-target coverage (proved; from cycloharvey Lemmas C, K′)
   167	
   168	Let τ be a target with modulus M (M = 1 when nothing is guessed), support I, |I| = K,
   169	e = max|k| ≤ d, window width W.
   170	
   171	- **(i) Degree e = 1, K = 2.** Lemma K′ (with K = 2) lets us write g = (Aq + Bp − c)/(C_d M²)
   172	  with integers A, B over the **fixed** denominator C_d M². Define H := max(|A|,|B|) =
   173	  C_d M²‖b‖_∞/P ≥ 1. This normalisation is also the one Thm B's count uses (critic,
   174	  finding 4).
   175	  Lemma C gives μ_τ ≤ C(W/‖b‖)^{1/2}, so
   176	
   177	    #covered integers ≤ Pμ_τ/M + C_I ≤ C·(PW/H)^{1/2} + C.        (1)
   178	
   179	  **The M-dependence cancels exactly.** That cancellation is the per-target form of
   180	  guess-neutrality.
   181	- **(ii) e = 1, K = 1** (support {1} or {−1}). Here ‖b‖ ≥ P/(C M), so the coverage is
   182	  ≤ C·W + C ≤ C(PW)^{1/2} + C when W ≤ P. If some W > P, then X ≥ √W > N^{1/4} already.
   183	- **(iii) e ≥ 2.** Lemma K′ gives ‖b‖ ≥ P^{e}/(C_d M^{K}), so
   184	
   185	    #covered ≤ C·M·P^{−e/K}W^{1/K}·P/M + C = C·P^{1−e/K}W^{1/K} + C.   (2)
   186	
   187	  Again M cancels.
   188	
   189	Targets with the same (M, class, vector) are merged and Lemma C is applied to the union of
   190	their windows. Lemma C only needs the measure, so the merged width is ≤ Σ W. "Distinct
   191	targets ≤ X" therefore refers to distinct triples.
   192	- The +C rounding term is paid once per *original* window, i.e. a preimage made of ≤ C_I
   193	  intervals.
   194	- The total is still ≤ CX, because every window costs at least one giant step.
   195	
   196	### 3.2 Theorem A (proved): 𝔐⁺ costs ≥ c·N^{1/6}
   197	
   198	**Statement.** Fix d and c > 1. Any 𝔐⁺ covering family of degree ≤ d has X ≥ c_{d,c}·N^{1/6}.
   199	
   200	*Proof.*
   201	- Coverage must reach c′P.
   202	- By (1) and (ii), the e = 1 targets cover at most
   203	  C P^{1/2} Σ_τ W_τ^{1/2} + CX ≤ C P^{1/2} X^{1/2}(Σ W)^{1/2} + CX ≤ C P^{1/2} X^{3/2} + CX
   204	  (Cauchy–Schwarz).
   205	- By (2) and Hölder over at most X targets of each of the finitely many (e, K) classes,
   206	  the e ≥ 2 targets cover at most C Σ_{(e,K)} P^{1−e/K} X^{1+1/K} + CX.
   207	- Put X = δP^{2/5} with δ ≤ 1. The (e,K) term is then
   208	  ≤ C δ^{1+1/K} P^{1 − (5e − 2K − 2)/(5K)} ≤ C δ P, because 5e ≥ 2K + 2 when K ≤ 2e and e ≥ 2.
   209	- So for X ≤ δP^{2/5} with δ small, the e ≥ 2 part covers ≤ c′P/2. The e = 1 part must then
   210	  give C P^{1/2}X^{3/2} ≥ c′P/2 − CX, i.e. X ≥ c P^{1/3} = c N^{1/6}.
   211	- If instead X > δP^{2/5}, then X ≥ δ N^{1/5} ≥ N^{1/6} for large N. ∎
   212	
   213	**Theorem A′ (proved).** If the e = 1 targets of an 𝔐⁺ family cover fewer than c′P/2
   214	candidates, then X ≥ c N^{1/5}, with any moduli. This is the second branch of the proof.
   215	
   216	**Tightness.** Equality in (1) with H = O(1) and W ≈ P/M² means a target covers its *whole*
   217	class across the range, P/M ≈ (PW)^{1/2} candidates. That is exactly what DCL's targets do
   218	(M ≈ N^{1/6}, W ≈ N^{1/6}). So A is tight up to the polylog/overlap factor, if coverage can
   219	be completed (Thm D, DCH).
   220	
   221	### 3.3 Theorem B (proved): the bound as a function of the number of moduli
   222	
   223	**Statement.** Suppose the e = 1 targets use J distinct moduli, all ≤ P (targets with M > P
   224	cover ≤ 1 + C candidates each and are absorbed in the +CX term). Then
   225	
   226	  X ≥ c·N^{1/5}·(J·N^{o(1)})^{−1/5}.
   227	
   228	*Proof.*
   229	- **Counting.** By cycloharvey §3.4 (e = 1 branch), for each modulus M and each (A,B), with
   230	  H measured over the fixed denominator C_d M² as in §3.1(i), the number of classes on
   231	  which (Aq + Bp − c)/(C_d M²) is integer-valued is ≤ O(1)^{ω(M)}·gcd(A,B,M).
   232	  - The critic brute-forced composite M ≤ 105 with denominators M² and 2M². The count is
   233	    always ≤ 4^{ω(M)}·gcd(A,B,M).
   234	  - Measured over the *reduced* denominator the count fails: M = 25, D = 125. So the number of
   235	  (class, vector) pairs with H ≤ h is ≤ M^{o(1)} h². Over J moduli it is ≤ J' h², with
   236	  J' = J·N^{o(1)}.
   237	- **Sum of 1/H.** For the ≤ X distinct triples, Σ_τ 1/H_τ ≤ C(J'X)^{1/2}: the extremal case
   238	  takes the X smallest heights, so h_max ≍ (X/J')^{1/2} and Σ ≍ J' h_max.
   239	- **Coverage.** Cauchy–Schwarz on (1) bounds the e = 1 coverage by
   240	  C P^{1/2}(Σ W)^{1/2}(Σ 1/H)^{1/2} + CX ≤ C P^{1/2} X (J'X)^{1/4} + CX.
   241	- **K = 1 targets.** Each covers ≤ CW + C (§3.1(ii)), so ≤ CX² + CX = o(P) in total when
   242	  X ≤ δP^{2/5}.
   243	- **Conclusion.** Theorem A's (e,K) computation handles the e ≥ 2 part. What remains is
   244	  X^{5/4} ≥ c P^{1/2}J'^{−1/4}. ∎
   245	
   246	**Corollary.**
   247	- J = 1 recovers cycloharvey's LB.
   248	- Exponent 1/5 − ε needs J ≥ N^{5ε−o(1)} moduli.
   249	- J ≤ X gives Theorem A again, up to N^{o(1)}.
   250	
   251	### 3.4 Theorem C (proved): CRT composes guess-neutrality multiplicatively
   252	
   253	**Statement.** Let M = M₁M₂ with gcd(M₁, M₂) = 1, let r be a class mod M, and let
   254	r_i = r mod M_i. Then
   255	
   256	  L_{r,M} = L_{r₁,M₁} + L_{r₂,M₂}  and  [L_{r,M} : Λ_I] = [L_{r₁,M₁} : Λ_I]·[L_{r₂,M₂} : Λ_I].
   257	
   258	*Proof.*
   259	- **Decomposition.** Elements of L_{r,M} lie in M^{−∞}Λ_I = Λ_I ⊗ Z[1/M]. Partial fractions
   260	  give M^{−∞}Λ/Λ = M₁^{−∞}Λ/Λ ⊕ M₂^{−∞}Λ/Λ, so g = g₁ + g₂ uniquely mod Λ.
   261	- **Each part is integer-valued.** Suppose g(p) − c ∈ Z for all p ≡ r (mod M). The
   262	  fractional part of g₁(p) lies in M₁^{−∞}Z/Z and depends only on p mod M₁^{a} for a large
   263	  (with q = N p^{−1} mod M₁^{a}). By CRT, every class mod M₁^{a} lying over r₁ contains
   264	  integers ≡ r₂ (mod M₂).
   265	- On such integers, g₂(p) has M₂-power denominator. The fractional part of g(p) − c is 0, so
   266	  the M₁-part g₁(p) − c₁ ≡ 0. Hence g₁ ∈ L_{r₁,M₁}, and likewise g₂ ∈ L_{r₂,M₂}.
   267	- **Converse.** If g₁ ∈ L_{r₁,M₁} and g₂ ∈ L_{r₂,M₂}, then g₁ + g₂ is integer-valued up to a
   268	  constant on the class r. So the sum is direct modulo Λ, and the indices multiply. ∎
   269	
   270	Experimental check: `experiments crt` compares a direct HNF computation of
   271	[L_{r,M₁M₂} : Λ] with the product of the two indices for I ∈ {(−1,1), (−2,−1,1)} and
   272	(M₁,M₂) ∈ {(5,7), (3,7), (5,11)} (§6, E-CRT).
   273	
   274	**Consequence (heuristic: it rests on cycloharvey's volume heuristic and the HNF-measured
   275	κ = K(K+1)/2; the rigorous single-modulus statement is cycloharvey's LB, neutral up to
   276	M^{−o(1)}).** Neutrality is
   277	the identity "index M^{K(K+1)/2} against φ(M) runs". Both sides are multiplicative over
   278	coprime parts, so within that heuristic a scheme covering **all** φ(M) classes of a composite M with a
   279	shared baby list is exactly neutral, whatever its CRT structure.
   280	- This includes CRT sign-splitting (signsplit's result) and the user's Gray-code bucketing:
   281	  odd-m_i bucketing costs more than it saves, and only the 2-adic r₀ ↦ r₀ + 4 identity gives
   282	  a constant.
   283	- *CRT does not break neutrality; it composes multiplicatively and stays neutral.*
   284	
   285	**What does break it.** DCL does not cover all classes of any modulus. For a fixed small v
   286	only the ρ_v(M) ∈ {0, 2} classes with a root are used; that is a 2/M fraction, but each is
   287	reached by a vector of height O(1) instead of the typical √M.
   288	- These "lucky" classes from N^{1/6+o(1)} coprime moduli are independent by CRT. They union
   289	  to almost everything (Thm D, proved), and conjecturally to everything (DCH).
   290	- In Thm B's terms, many moduli supply J·h² cheap (class, vector) pairs, where one modulus
   291	  supplies only h².
   292	
   293	### 3.5 Theorem D (proved, in the search model): average-case N^{1/6+ε}
   294	
   295	**Statement.** Fix ε ∈ (0, 1/10). For n-bit balanced semiprimes N = pq (primes p < q ≤ 2p),
   296	take the following design. **H₀, Y and m depend on n only**, so the sifted classes for a
   297	fixed q do not move with p (critic, finding 7).
   298	- V = {(A,B) : gcd(A,B) = 1, A, B ≤ H₀} with H₀ = ⌈2^{nε/2}·n⌉;
   299	- 𝒫 = the primes in (Y, 2Y] with Y = ⌈2^{n/6}H₀^{−1/3}⌉;
   300	- every root r of Br² ≡ AN (mod ℓ), for each v ∈ V and ℓ ∈ 𝒫;
   301	- m = ⌈C H₀ 2^{n/2}/Y²⌉.
   302	
   303	Its model cost is O(H₀^{5/3}N^{1/6}) = N^{1/6+5ε/6+o(1)}. The fraction of balanced semiprimes
   304	whose p is covered by no target is O(N^{−ε}).
   305	
   306	*Proof.*
   307	- **Cost.** At most 2|V||𝒫| targets with ⌈W/m⌉ = 1, by §2.1 with W ≤ C H₀√N/Y² + 1. So the
   308	  cost is m + 2|V||𝒫| ≤ C H₀√N/Y² + 4H₀² Y/log Y = O(H₀^{5/3} N^{1/6}).
   309	- **Reduction to a sieve.** Fix a prime q. The admissible p form an interval I_q of length
   310	  ≍ √N (p ∈ [q/2, q] with pq of n bits). By §2.2, p is covered iff ∃(v, ℓ) with
   311	  ℓ | Bp − Aq. For ℓ ∈ 𝒫 we have ℓ ∤ q (ℓ < q), so this is p ≡ Aq·B^{−1} (mod ℓ).
   312	- **Distinct classes.** For ℓ > 2H₀² the slopes A/B are distinct mod ℓ, since
   313	  |AB' − A'B| < H₀² < ℓ. So the uncovered p avoid ω(ℓ) = |V| residue classes modulo every
   314	  ℓ ∈ 𝒫.
   315	- **Large sieve.** The arithmetic large sieve (Montgomery 1968; Montgomery–Vaughan 1973)
   316	  gives
   317	
