   404	### PROVED: the requested average bound in a precise local model
   405	
   406	The additional geometric restriction below is useful because it allows \(N\)-dependent algebraic windows while excluding arbitrary computational information hidden in their endpoints.
   407	
   408	**Theorem 3 — residue-local algebraic-window bound.** Fix constants controlling the balanced range and a semialgebraic complexity bound \(D\). Consider an \(n\)-dependent schedule with \(T\le X\) slots. Slot \(\tau\) has:
   409	
   410	1. A modulus \(1\le M_\tau\le P\), fixed before seeing \(N\).
   411	2. For every unit residue \(s\bmod M_\tau\), one unit class \(r_\tau(s)\).
   412	3. A real region \(R_{\tau,s}\) in the balanced \((p,q)\)-box, described by at most \(D\) polynomial equalities/inequalities of degree at most \(D\).
   413	4. A width cap \(w_\tau\ge1\), independent of \(s\), with
   414	   \[
   415	   \sum_\tau w_\tau\le X^2.
   416	   \]
   417	5. For every real product value \(u\asymp P^2\), the fiber bound
   418	   \[
   419	   \operatorname{length}
   420	   \{x:(x,u/x)\in R_{\tau,s}\}
   421	   \le C_D M_\tau\bigl(\sqrt{Pw_\tau}+w_\tau\bigr). \tag{*}
   422	   \]
   423	
   424	A declared hit requires
   425	\[
   426	s=pq\bmod M_\tau,\qquad
   427	p\equiv r_\tau(s)\pmod {M_\tau},\qquad
   428	(p,q)\in R_{\tau,s}.
   429	\]
   430	
   431	Then the fraction of balanced prime pairs hit by the schedule is at most
   432	\[
   433	C_D(\log P)^2
   434	\left(
   435	X^{3/2}P^{-1/2}+\frac{X^2}{P}
   436	\right).
   437	\]
   438	
   439	The same conclusion allows polylogarithmically many coarse archimedean cases, with an additional polylogarithmic factor.
   440	
   441	**Proof.** Fix one slot, abbreviating \(M=M_\tau,w=w_\tau\).
   442	
   443	For each unit \(s\), the congruences force
   444	\[
   445	p\equiv r(s)\pmod M,\qquad
   446	q\equiv s\,r(s)^{-1}\pmod M.
   447	\]
   448	Thus the relevant points lie in one translate of \(M\mathbb Z^2\).
   449	
   450	The change of coordinates \((p,q)\mapsto(u=pq,p)\) has area element
   451	\[
   452	dp\,dq=\frac{du\,dp}{p}.
   453	\]
   454	Because \(p\asymp P\), the fiber estimate (*) gives
   455	\[
   456	\operatorname{area}(R_s)
   457	\le C_D M\bigl(P^{3/2}\sqrt w+Pw\bigr). \tag{1}
   458	\]
   459	
   460	For a bounded-complexity semialgebraic region in a box of side \(O(P)\),
   461	\[
   462	\#\bigl(R_s\cap((a,b)+M\mathbb Z^2)\bigr)
   463	\le
   464	\frac{\operatorname{area}(R_s)}{M^2}
   465	+C_D(P/M+1). \tag{2}
   466	\]
   467	To see (2), partition the boundary into \(O_D(1)\) coordinate-monotone algebraic arcs and isolated points. These meet \(O_D(P/M+1)\) lattice cells; fully interior cells contribute their area divided by \(M^2\).
   468	
   469	There are at most \(M\) residues \(s\). Summing (1)–(2), and using \(M\le P\), bounds the slot’s covered integer pairs by
   470	\[
   471	C_D\bigl(P^{3/2}\sqrt w+Pw+P\bigr).
   472	\]
   473	The prime-pair population has size \(\gg P^2/(\log P)^2\), by the prime number theorem in fixed proportional intervals. Consequently, the covered fraction is at most
   474	\[
   475	C_D(\log P)^2
   476	\left(
   477	P^{-1/2}\sum_\tau\sqrt{w_\tau}
   478	+P^{-1}\sum_\tau w_\tau+\frac TP
   479	\right).
   480	\]
   481	Finally,
   482	\[
   483	\sum_\tau\sqrt{w_\tau}\le\sqrt{T\sum_\tau w_\tau}\le X^{3/2},
   484	\]
   485	and \(T/P\le X^2/P\). ∎
   486	
   487	This proof uses **integer-pair upper bounds**. It requires no equidistribution theorem for primes in the relevant progressions.
   488	
   489	### How this relates to Laurent targets
   490	
   491	For the two-term HH targets, the archimedean estimate behind Theorem A gives
   492	\[
   493	\text{fiber length}\le C M\sqrt{Pw/H}
   494	\le C M\sqrt{Pw}.
   495	\]
   496	One-term targets contribute the \(CMw\) alternative.
   497	
   498	For higher-degree targets with a uniform real-fiber quantization bound,
   499	\[
   500	CM P^{1-e/K}w^{1/K},
   501	\]
   502	the same argument gives Corollary 2.
   503	
   504	Thus Theorem 3 applies when residue-local coefficient choices and window endpoints produce bounded-complexity algebraic regions—for example, endpoints obtained from fixed rational or algebraic formulas in \(N\), with harmless rounding enlargements.
   505	
   506	Its limitations are explicit:
   507	
   508	- fixed slots and moduli;
   509	- moduli at most \(P\);
   510	- uniform width caps;
   511	- controlled algebraic complexity;
   512	- success through a declared target.
   513	
   514	I have **not** proved the same bound from only a per-input width budget, arbitrary adaptive choices of moduli, or unrestricted endpoint computations.
   515	
   516	For
   517	\[
   518	X=N^{1/6-\delta},
   519	\]
   520	the theorem gives a success fraction at most
   521	\[
   522	(\log N)^{O(1)}
   523	\left(N^{-3\delta/2}+N^{-1/6-2\delta}\right)=o(1).
   524	\]
   525	Within this model, beating \(1/6\) loses even constant-fraction success.
   526	
   527	### SPECULATIVE: gcd verification and list decoding
   528	
   529	Gcd verification is cheap, but it does not by itself enlarge the certified coverage set.
   530	
   531	Accidental collisions can enlarge actual success. For example, a relation caused by unexpectedly small \(\operatorname{ord}_p(\beta)\) need not correspond to any declared Laurent window. Theorem 3 does not bound such successes. Extending it would require average control of those order relations.
   532	
   533	Similarly, many partial collisions might identify factor bits through a decoding procedure. The missing mechanism is a decoder that both:
   534	
   535	- extracts sufficiently concentrated information about \(p\);
   536	- costs less than the saving obtained from that information.
   537	
   538	A list of many scattered possibilities is not automatically equivalent to one short interval.
   539	
   540	### PROVED calculation: the value of a short-span selector
   541	
   542	Suppose a uniform public procedure, costing \(S(N)\), returns an interval of length
   543	\[
   544	R=N^{1/2-\eta}
   545	\]
   546	containing the true factor on almost all inputs.
   547	
   548	For bounded weights, the variation of \(Bp+AN/p\) on this interval is \(O(HR)\). The DCL search-cost calculation becomes
   549	\[
   550	\widetilde O\!\left(\frac{HR}{Y^2}+H^2Y\right).
   551	\]
   552	Choosing \(Y\asymp(R/H)^{1/3}\) gives
   553	\[
   554	\widetilde O(H^{5/3}R^{1/3})
   555	=N^{1/6-\eta/3}(\log N)^{O(1)}
   556	\]
   557	for polylogarithmic \(H\). The selector cost \(S(N)\) must be added. This establishes the claimed search saving; coverage and one-sided extraction must also be maintained.
   558	
   559	There is no known equivalence between computing such a selector for an arbitrary fixed \(0<\eta<1/4\) and polynomial-time factoring. Factoring supplies a selector immediately; the converse at that weak precision is unproved.
   560	
   561	## 6. Other mechanisms and the most useful remaining questions
   562	
   563	### KNOWN: lattice recovery from partial factor information
   564	
   565	Coppersmith’s method is a genuinely different mechanism. Sufficiently accurate approximation to \(p\)—safely, an interval of length \(N^{1/4-\epsilon}\)—permits deterministic polynomial-time recovery for fixed \(\epsilon>0\). It uses lattice reduction, not a sumset collision search. [Coppersmith, 1997](https://nymity.ch/anomalous-tor-keys/bibliography/pdf/Coppersmith1997a.pdf)
   566	
   567	Thus a sufficiently strong public selector would already yield polynomial-time factoring. A weaker selector could still improve the collision exponent without any known polynomial-time equivalence.
   568	
   569	### HEURISTIC: smooth-order annihilation
   570	
   571	ECM replaces a short unknown exponent by a smooth annihilating exponent. Under a suitable joint smoothness heuristic for a deterministic curve schedule, it also predicts subexponential time. Its unresolved ingredient is the joint hitting statement described in Section 4, rather than Laurent flatness.
   572	
   573	### SPECULATIVE: precise questions worth pursuing
   574	
   575	The remaining questions I would prioritize are:
   576	
   577	1. **Smooth-relation concentration over balanced semiprimes:** establish H1 for an explicit short deterministic schedule, together with a nontrivial dependency character. This would bypass the collision barrier entirely.
   578	2. **Succinct-power projected boxes:** prove a coverage bound using image cardinality without replacing a sparse sum of arithmetic progressions by its potentially enormous interval hull.
   579	3. **Useful giant multiplicity:** construct or bound polynomially many distinct certified identities per stored giant, charging exponent construction.
   580	4. **Remove restrictions from Theorem 3:** replace uniform width caps by the actual per-input budget, and handle adaptive moduli or more general computable endpoints.

